  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nA stubborn layer of stratus has been slowly eroding across the\nlocal area this afternoon. Central areas have seen little to no\nsunshine, helping keep temperatures stuck mostly in the lower \n40s. This stratus should continue to erode through the late \nafternoon/early evening hours, with mostly clear skies\neventually returning to the area overnight. \n\nWith mostly clear skies and light winds tonight, expect \ntemperatures to fall rapidly overnight, with a cold/frosty start\nto the day across the local area to finish out the work week.\nThis will likely be the coolest day of the fall, thus far, with\nwidespread lower to mid 20s anticipated. \n\nThis cool/dry weather is then anticipated to persist into at \nleast Saturday evening, when the next disturbance crossing the \nsouthern plains clips southern portions of the local area. This \nsystem has continued to trend south over the past 24 hours, and \nconsequently now have pops mostly limited to portions of north \ncentral Kansas, with little to no precipitation accumulation \nanticipated.\n\nBehind this initial system, high pressure aloft will build back\nacross the plains from the southwest to start the new work week,\nwith a rebound in temperatures to slightly above normal levels \nfor Tuesday and Wednesday as breezy southerly winds also\nincrease across the local area. The next system is then forecast\nto lift out of the southwest and into the plains - midweek -\nbrining a small chance for some light precip Wednesday into\nearly Thursday. \n\nStill plenty of uncertainty with the following upper level low\nas is develops across the southwest late in the week, but many\nensemble members indicate this could be another fairly wet\nsystem for the following weekend with temperatures suggesting\na cold rain across the local area as the system intensifies and\nmoves into the plains.",
